---
code: computed-source-operand
when-fires: a BOOK's `.`/`source` operand carries a command substitution or an arithmetic expansion, so its value is something only the target can produce and the load plane cannot name the file the `.` loads. analysis/funcenv.rs `evaluate_word` records the cause, cli/main.rs `load_head_notices` mints it, one per `.` line, spanned there; the same cause raises the run's outcome to `EXIT_LOAD_UNRESOLVABLE`, whole-run and pre-network. World-as-payload: the mint sits in the binary's own load-edge driver, past everything the in-process book pipeline composes.
why: `30P:rul-floor-valid-text-never-parse-fails` (TYPED 2026-08-22) moved this outcome's TIER and kept the outcome. `. "$(dirname "$0")/helpers.sh"` parses and runs under `posh ∩ dash`, so the parser refusing it — which is what happened until now — threw away a whole run's diagnosis and aid over text the floor accepts. The site itself is an ordinary point havoc (`principle-unknown-source-is-a-point-havoc`): bindings unknown AT that line, the file not acquired, the load walls. What survives is the pre-network fail-fast, because a `dorc … && deploy` chain must still stop: the plan Dorc can build over a book whose dependencies it cannot name is much poorer than the author expects, and silently proceeding would spend the reader's attention on it. The sanctioned repair is `rul-static-predict-sites-loads` — an authored, statically-evaluable `dirname__predict` whose stdout is positively claimed, which the stdlib does not yet carry — and until then the EXACT spellings are `${0%/*}` and the `case $0 in */*)` fold. Remediation register (help) wanted: name those spellings, and say that the artifact still ships. The softer UX (per-site rather than whole-run, or no fail-fast at all) is the human's to rule; the machinery here handles the worst case either way.
---
-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
. "$(hork)/helpers.sh"

-- replay --
$ dorc plan --book=book.sh > /dev/null
loading: error[computed-source-operand]: [unwritten: computed-source-operand]
      [ book.sh:2:1
   2 | . "$(hork)/helpers.sh"
     | \____________________/
classify: note[cmdsub-operand-top]: operand 1 is a command-substitution `$(...)`
/ arithmetic / operator-form expansion, so Dorc cannot know its value until the
command runs on the host -- there is nothing to resolve and no read-only probe
to check. Dorc elides a command only when it can prove the command's effect is
already in place, so this one is left to run on every apply.
      [ book.sh:2:3
   2 | . "$(hork)/helpers.sh"
     |   \__________________/

   = repair:  Give the operand a value Dorc can resolve statically -- a literal,
              or a variable assigned from one -- so Dorc can probe it and elide
              the command once it has converged. If the value must stay dynamic,
              load an oracle that vouches for this command's convergence, and
              Dorc will guard the command instead of running it every time.
classify: note[cmdsub-inner-nonleaf]: Dorc cannot elide `hork` on its own: it
runs inside a `$(...)` substitution as part of evaluating its enclosing line, so
it runs whenever that line does.
      [ book.sh:1:1
   1 | #!/bin/sh
     | \__/
probe: note[site-unresolvable]: sm 1 site run unprobed (no read-only check could
be shipped): `. "$(hork)/helpers.sh"` -- run `dorc why` for the per-site detail
(the apply runs each anyway, to stay safe)
      [ book.sh:2:1
   2 | . "$(hork)/helpers.sh"
     | \____________________/

   = help:  sm site runs `. "$(hork)/helpers.sh"`
why: ran because operand 1 is a command-substitution `$(...)` or runtime-dynamic value -- its value couldn't be resolved (first seen at 2:1 `. "$(hork)/helpers.sh"`); so dorc runs it, to stay safe (when unsure, run). to skip it, make the operand a literal Dorc can resolve+probe [resolve-dynamism]
hint: '.' (line 2) is unmodeled: it is the first wall -- an oracle vouching its convergence would elide it when converged
dorc: run `dorc why` for the per-site cause-chains, or `dorc why book.sh:N` to query a source line
dorc: plan-summary sites=1 elide=0 omit=0 guard=0 run=1 may-alias=0
dorc: decision-digest f59a6c024d4cd71171bdd9ea400ac1781005189315ec85eb7e2911f6b097b2ba
